(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("soph-b",[{"sp":"N","t":"Theodorus, Theaetetus, Socrates. An Eleatic Stranger, whom Theodorus and Theaetetus bring with them. The younger Socrates, who is a silent auditor.","st":"216a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theodorus:<\/span> Here we are, Socrates, true to our agreement of yesterday; and we bring with us a stranger from Elea, who is a disciple of Parmenides and Zeno, and a true philosopher.","st":"216a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Is he not rather a god, Theodorus, who comes to us in the disguise of a stranger? For Homer says that all the gods, and especially the god of strangers, are companions of the meek and just, and visit the good and evil among men. And may not your companion be one of those higher powers, a cross-examining deity, who has come to spy out our weakness in argument, and to cross-examine us?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theodorus:<\/span> Nay, Socrates, he is not one of the disputatious sort — he is too good for that. And, in my opinion, he is not a god at all; but divine he certainly is, for this is a title which I should give to all philosophers.","st":"216b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Capital, my friend! and I may add that they are almost as hard to be discerned as the gods. For the true philosophers, and such as are not merely made up for the occasion, appear in various forms unrecognized by the ignorance of men, and they ‘hover about cities,’ as Homer declares, looking from above upon human life; and some think nothing of them, and others can never think enough; and sometimes they appear as statesmen, and sometimes as sophists; and then, again, to many they seem to be no better than madmen. I should like to ask our Eleatic friend, if he would tell us, what is thought about them in Italy, and to whom the terms are applied.","st":"216c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theodorus:<\/span> What terms?","st":"216c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Sophist, statesman, philosopher.","st":"216c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theodorus:<\/span> What is your difficulty about them, and what made you ask?","st":"216c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I want to know whether by his countrymen they are regarded as one or two; or do they, as the names are three, distinguish also three kinds, and assign one to each name?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theodorus:<\/span> I dare say that the Stranger will not object to discuss the question. What do you say, Stranger?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I am far from objecting, Theodorus, nor have I any difficulty in replying that by us they are regarded as three. But to define precisely the nature of each of them is by no means a slight or easy task.","st":"217b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theodorus:<\/span> You have happened to light, Socrates, almost on the very question which we were asking our friend before we came hither, and he excused himself to us, as he does now to you; although he admitted that the matter had been fully discussed, and that he remembered the answer.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then do not, Stranger, deny us the first favour which we ask of you: I am sure that you will not, and therefore I shall only beg of you to say whether you like and are accustomed to make a long oration on a subject which you want to explain to another, or to proceed by the method of question and answer. I remember hearing a very noble discussion in which Parmenides employed the latter of the two methods, when I was a young man, and he was far advanced in years. (Compare Parm.)","st":"217c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I prefer to talk with another when he responds pleasantly, and is light in hand; if not, I would rather have my own say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Any one of the present company will respond kindly to you, and you can choose whom you like of them; I should recommend you to take a young person — Theaetetus, for example — unless you have a preference for some one else.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I feel ashamed, Socrates, being a new-comer into your society, instead of talking a little and hearing others talk, to be spinning out a long soliloquy or address, as if I wanted to show off. For the true answer will certainly be a very long one, a great deal longer than might be expected from such a short and simple question. At the same time, I fear that I may seem rude and ungracious if I refuse your courteous request, especially after what you have said. For I certainly cannot object to your proposal, that Theaetetus should respond, having already conversed with him myself, and being recommended by you to take him.","st":"217d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> But are you sure, Stranger, that this will be quite so acceptable to the rest of the company as Socrates imagines?","st":"217d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You hear them applauding, Theaetetus; after that, there is nothing more to be said. Well then, I am to argue with you, and if you tire of the argument, you may complain of your friends and not of me.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I do not think that I shall tire, and if I do, I shall get my friend here, young Socrates, the namesake of the elder Socrates, to help; he is about my own age, and my partner at the gymnasium, and is constantly accustomed to work with me.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Very good; you can decide about that for yourself as we proceed. Meanwhile you and I will begin together and enquire into the nature of the Sophist, first of the three: I should like you to make out what he is and bring him to light in a discussion; for at present we are only agreed about the name, but of the thing to which we both apply the name possibly you have one notion and I another; whereas we ought always to come to an understanding about the thing itself in terms of a definition, and not merely about the name minus the definition. Now the tribe of Sophists which we are investigating is not easily caught or defined; and the world has long ago agreed, that if great subjects are to be adequately treated, they must be studied in the lesser and easier instances of them before we proceed to the greatest of all. And as I know that the tribe of Sophists is troublesome and hard to be caught, I should recommend that we practise beforehand the method which is to be applied to him on some simple and smaller thing, unless you can suggest a better way.","st":"218a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Indeed I cannot.","st":"218a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then suppose that we work out some lesser example which will be a pattern of the greater?","st":"218a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Good.","st":"218d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> What is there which is well known and not great, and is yet as susceptible of definition as any larger thing? Shall I say an angler? He is familiar to all of us, and not a very interesting or important person.","st":"218e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> He is not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yet I suspect that he will furnish us with the sort of definition and line of enquiry which we want.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us begin by asking whether he is a man having art or not having art, but some other power.","st":"219a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> He is clearly a man of art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of arts there are two kinds?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is agriculture, and the tending of mortal creatures, and the art of constructing or moulding vessels, and there is the art of imitation — all these may be appropriately called by a single name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean? And what is the name?","st":"219b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> He who brings into existence something that did not exist before is said to be a producer, and that which is brought into existence is said to be produced.","st":"219b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And all the arts which were just now mentioned are characterized by this power of producing?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They are.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then let us sum them up under the name of productive or creative art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Next follows the whole class of learning and cognition; then comes trade, fighting, hunting. And since none of these produces anything, but is only engaged in conquering by word or deed, or in preventing others from conquering, things which exist and have been already produced — in each and all of these branches there appears to be an art which may be called acquisitive.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, that is the proper name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Seeing, then, that all arts are either acquisitive or creative, in which class shall we place the art of the angler?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Clearly in the acquisitive class.","st":"219d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the acquisitive may be subdivided into two parts: there is exchange, which is voluntary and is effected by gifts, hire, purchase; and the other part of acquisitive, which takes by force of word or deed, may be termed conquest?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is implied in what has been said.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And may not conquest be again subdivided?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Open force may be called fighting, and secret force may have the general name of hunting?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there is no reason why the art of hunting should not be further divided.","st":"219e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How would you make the division?","st":"219e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Into the hunting of living and of lifeless prey.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, if both kinds exist.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Of course they exist; but the hunting after lifeless things having no special name, except some sorts of diving, and other small matters, may be omitted; the hunting after living things may be called animal hunting.","st":"220a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"220a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And animal hunting may be truly said to have two divisions, land-animal hunting, which has many kinds and names, and water-animal hunting, or the hunting after animals who swim?","st":"220a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of swimming animals, one class lives on the wing and the other in the water?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Fowling is the general term under which the hunting of all birds is included.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"220b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The hunting of animals who live in the water has the general name of fishing.","st":"220b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And this sort of hunting may be further divided also into two principal kinds?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is one kind which takes them in nets, another which takes them by a blow.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean, and how do you distinguish them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> As to the first kind — all that surrounds and encloses anything to prevent egress, may be rightly called an enclosure.","st":"220c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> For which reason twig baskets, casting-nets, nooses, creels, and the like may all be termed ‘enclosures’?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And therefore this first kind of capture may be called by us capture with enclosures, or something of that sort?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The other kind, which is practised by a blow with hooks and three-pronged spears, when summed up under one name, may be called striking, unless you, Theaetetus, can find some better name?","st":"220d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Never mind the name — what you suggest will do very well.","st":"220d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is one mode of striking, which is done at night, and by the light of a fire, and is by the hunters themselves called firing, or spearing by firelight.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the fishing by day is called by the general name of barbing, because the spears, too, are barbed at the point.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, that is the term.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Of this barb-fishing, that which strikes the fish who is below from above is called spearing, because this is the way in which the three-pronged spears are mostly used.","st":"220e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, it is often called so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then now there is only one kind remaining.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is that?","st":"221a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When a hook is used, and the fish is not struck in any chance part of his body, as he is with the spear, but only about the head and mouth, and is then drawn out from below upwards with reeds and rods: — What is the right name of that mode of fishing, Theaetetus?","st":"221a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I suspect that we have now discovered the object of our search.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then now you and I have come to an understanding not only about the name of the angler’s art, but about the definition of the thing itself. One half of all art was acquisitive — half of the acquisitive art was conquest or taking by force, half of this was hunting, and half of hunting was hunting animals, half of this was hunting water animals — of this again, the under half was fishing, half of fishing was striking; a part of striking was fishing with a barb, and one half of this again, being the kind which strikes with a hook and draws the fish from below upwards, is the art which we have been seeking, and which from the nature of the operation is denoted angling or drawing up (aspalieutike, anaspasthai).","st":"221b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The result has been quite satisfactorily brought out.","st":"221c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now, following this pattern, let us endeavour to find out what a Sophist is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> By all means.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The first question about the angler was, whether he was a skilled artist or unskilled?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And shall we call our new friend unskilled, or a thorough master of his craft?","st":"221d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not unskilled, for his name, as, indeed, you imply, must surely express his nature.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then he must be supposed to have some art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> By heaven, they are cousins! it never occurred to us.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Who are cousins?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The angler and the Sophist.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> In what way are they related?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> They both appear to me to be hunters.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How the Sophist? Of the other we have spoken.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You remember our division of hunting, into hunting after swimming animals and land animals?","st":"221e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And you remember that we subdivided the swimming and left the land animals, saying that there were many kinds of them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Thus far, then, the Sophist and the angler, starting from the art of acquiring, take the same road?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> So it would appear.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Their paths diverge when they reach the art of animal hunting; the one going to the sea-shore, and to the rivers and to the lakes, and angling for the animals which are in them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> While the other goes to land and water of another sort — rivers of wealth and broad meadow-lands of generous youth; and he also is intending to take the animals which are in them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Of hunting on land there are two principal divisions.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"222c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> One is the hunting of tame, and the other of wild animals.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> But are tame animals ever hunted?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, if you include man under tame animals. But if you like you may say that there are no tame animals, or that, if there are, man is not among them; or you may say that man is a tame animal but is not hunted — you shall decide which of these alternatives you prefer.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I should say, Stranger, that man is a tame animal, and I admit that he is hunted.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then let us divide the hunting of tame animals into two parts.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How shall we make the division?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us define piracy, man-stealing, tyranny, the whole military art, by one name, as hunting with violence.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"222d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But the art of the lawyer, of the popular orator, and the art of conversation may be called in one word the art of persuasion.","st":"222d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of persuasion, there may be said to be two kinds?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> One is private, and the other public.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; each of them forms a class.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of private hunting, one sort receives hire, and the other brings gifts.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I do not understand you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You seem never to have observed the manner in which lovers hunt.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To what do you refer?","st":"222e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I mean that they lavish gifts on those whom they hunt in addition to other inducements.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us admit this, then, to be the amatory art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But that sort of hireling whose conversation is pleasing and who baits his hook only with pleasure and exacts nothing but his maintenance in return, we should all, if I am not mistaken, describe as possessing flattery or an art of making things pleasant.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"223a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And that sort, which professes to form acquaintances only for the sake of virtue, and demands a reward in the shape of money, may be fairly called by another name?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what is the name? Will you tell me?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> It is obvious enough; for I believe that we have discovered the Sophist: which is, as I conceive, the proper name for the class described.","st":"223b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then now, Theaetetus, his art may be traced as a branch of the appropriative, acquisitive family — which hunts animals, — living — land — tame animals; which hunts man, — privately — for hire, — taking money in exchange — having the semblance of education; and this is termed Sophistry, and is a hunt after young men of wealth and rank — such is the conclusion.","st":"223b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Just so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us take another branch of his genealogy; for he is a professor of a great and many-sided art; and if we look back at what has preceded we see that he presents another aspect, besides that of which we are speaking.","st":"223c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> In what respect?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There were two sorts of acquisitive art; the one concerned with hunting, the other with exchange.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There were.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of the art of exchange there are two divisions, the one of giving, and the other of selling.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Let us assume that.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Next, we will suppose the art of selling to be divided into two parts.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is one part which is distinguished as the sale of a man’s own productions; another, which is the exchange of the works of others.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is not that part of exchange which takes place in the city, being about half of the whole, termed retailing?","st":"223e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"223e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And that which exchanges the goods of one city for those of another by selling and buying is the exchange of the merchant?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And you are aware that this exchange of the merchant is of two kinds: it is partly concerned with food for the use of the body, and partly with the food of the soul which is bartered and received in exchange for money.","st":"224a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"224a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You want to know what is the meaning of food for the soul; the other kind you surely understand.","st":"224a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"224a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Take music in general and painting and marionette playing and many other things, which are purchased in one city, and carried away and sold in another — wares of the soul which are hawked about either for the sake of instruction or amusement; — may not he who takes them about and sells them be quite as truly called a merchant as he who sells meats and drinks?","st":"224b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure he may.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And would you not call by the same name him who buys up knowledge and goes about from city to city exchanging his wares for money?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly I should.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Of this merchandise of the soul, may not one part be fairly termed the art of display? And there is another part which is certainly not less ridiculous, but being a trade in learning must be called by some name germane to the matter?","st":"224c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The latter should have two names, — one descriptive of the sale of the knowledge of virtue, and the other of the sale of other kinds of knowledge.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The name of art-seller corresponds well enough to the latter; but you must try and tell me the name of the other.","st":"224d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> He must be the Sophist, whom we are seeking; no other name can possibly be right.","st":"224d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> No other; and so this trader in virtue again turns out to be our friend the Sophist, whose art may now be traced from the art of acquisition through exchange, trade, merchandise, to a merchandise of the soul which is concerned with speech and the knowledge of virtue.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"224e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there may be a third reappearance of him; — for he may have settled down in a city, and may fabricate as well as buy these same wares, intending to live by selling them, and he would still be called a Sophist?","st":"224e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then that part of the acquisitive art which exchanges, and of exchange which either sells a man’s own productions or retails those of others, as the case may be, and in either way sells the knowledge of virtue, you would again term Sophistry?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I must, if I am to keep pace with the argument.","st":"225a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us consider once more whether there may not be yet another aspect of sophistry.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In the acquisitive there was a subdivision of the combative or fighting art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There was.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Perhaps we had better divide it.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What shall be the divisions?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There shall be one division of the competitive, and another of the pugnacious.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> That part of the pugnacious which is a contest of bodily strength may be properly called by some such name as violent.","st":"225b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And when the war is one of words, it may be termed controversy?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And controversy may be of two kinds.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When long speeches are answered by long speeches, and there is public discussion about the just and unjust, that is forensic controversy.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there is a private sort of controversy, which is cut up into questions and answers, and this is commonly called disputation?","st":"225c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, that is the name.","st":"225c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of disputation, that sort which is only a discussion about contracts, and is carried on at random, and without rules of art, is recognized by the reasoning faculty to be a distinct class, but has hitherto had no distinctive name, and does not deserve to receive one from us.","st":"225c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> No; for the different sorts of it are too minute and heterogeneous.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But that which proceeds by rules of art to dispute about justice and injustice in their own nature, and about things in general, we have been accustomed to call argumentation (Eristic)?","st":"225d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of argumentation, one sort wastes money, and the other makes money.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Suppose we try and give to each of these two classes a name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Let us do so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I should say that the habit which leads a man to neglect his own affairs for the pleasure of conversation, of which the style is far from being agreeable to the majority of his hearers, may be fairly termed loquacity: such is my opinion.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is the common name for it.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But now who the other is, who makes money out of private disputation, it is your turn to say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There is only one true answer: he is the wonderful Sophist, of whom we are in pursuit, and who reappears again for the fourth time.","st":"226a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, and with a fresh pedigree, for he is the money-making species of the Eristic, disputatious, controversial, pugnacious, combative, acquisitive family, as the argument has already proven.","st":"226a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> How true was the observation that he was a many-sided animal, and not to be caught with one hand, as they say!","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Then you must catch him with two.","st":"226b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, we must, if we can. And therefore let us try another track in our pursuit of him: You are aware that there are certain menial occupations which have names among servants?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, there are many such; which of them do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I mean such as sifting, straining, winnowing, threshing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And besides these there are a great many more, such as carding, spinning, adjusting the warp and the woof; and thousands of similar expressions are used in the arts.","st":"226c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of what are they to be patterns, and what are we going to do with them all?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I think that in all of these there is implied a notion of division.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then if, as I was saying, there is one art which includes all of them, ought not that art to have one name?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And what is the name of the art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The art of discerning or discriminating.","st":"226d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"226d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Think whether you cannot divide this.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I should have to think a long while.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In all the previously named processes either like has been separated from like or the better from the worse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I see now what you mean.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is no name for the first kind of separation; of the second, which throws away the worse and preserves the better, I do know a name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Every discernment or discrimination of that kind, as I have observed, is called a purification.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, that is the usual expression.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And any one may see that purification is of two kinds.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Perhaps so, if he were allowed time to think; but I do not see at this moment.","st":"227a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There are many purifications of bodies which may with propriety be comprehended under a single name.","st":"227a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they, and what is their name?","st":"227a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is the purification of living bodies in their inward and in their outward parts, of which the former is duly effected by medicine and gymnastic, the latter by the not very dignified art of the bath-man; and there is the purification of inanimate substances — to this the arts of fulling and of furbishing in general attend in a number of minute particulars, having a variety of names which are thought ridiculous.","st":"227b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"227b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There can be no doubt that they are thought ridiculous, Theaetetus; but then the dialectical art never considers whether the benefit to be derived from the purge is greater or less than that to be derived from the sponge, and has not more interest in the one than in the other; her endeavour is to know what is and is not kindred in all arts, with a view to the acquisition of intelligence; and having this in view, she honours them all alike, and when she makes comparisons, she counts one of them not a whit more ridiculous than another; nor does she esteem him who adduces as his example of hunting, the general’s art, at all more decorous than another who cites that of the vermin-destroyer, but only as the greater pretender of the two. And as to your question concerning the name which was to comprehend all these arts of purification, whether of animate or inanimate bodies, the art of dialectic is in no wise particular about fine words, if she may be only allowed to have a general name for all other purifications, binding them up together and separating them off from the purification of the soul or intellect. For this is the purification at which she wants to arrive, and this we should understand to be her aim.","st":"227b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, I understand; and I agree that there are two sorts of purification, and that one of them is concerned with the soul, and that there is another which is concerned with the body.","st":"227d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Excellent; and now listen to what I am going to say, and try to divide further the first of the two.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Whatever line of division you suggest, I will endeavour to assist you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do we admit that virtue is distinct from vice in the soul?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And purification was to leave the good and to cast out whatever is bad?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then any taking away of evil from the soul may be properly called purification?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"228a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And in the soul there are two kinds of evil.","st":"228a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"228a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The one may be compared to disease in the body, the other to deformity.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I do not understand.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Perhaps you have never reflected that disease and discord are the same.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To this, again, I know not what I should reply.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you not conceive discord to be a dissolution of kindred elements, originating in some disagreement?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Just that.","st":"228b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is deformity anything but the want of measure, which is always unsightly?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Exactly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And do we not see that opinion is opposed to desire, pleasure to anger, reason to pain, and that all these elements are opposed to one another in the souls of bad men?","st":"228c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"228c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet they must all be akin?","st":"228c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"228c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we shall be right in calling vice a discord and disease of the soul?","st":"228c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And when things having motion, and aiming at an appointed mark, continually miss their aim and glance aside, shall we say that this is the effect of symmetry among them, or of the want of symmetry?","st":"228d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Clearly of the want of symmetry.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But surely we know that no soul is voluntarily ignorant of anything?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what is ignorance but the aberration of a mind which is bent on truth, and in which the process of understanding is perverted?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we are to regard an unintelligent soul as deformed and devoid of symmetry?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"228e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then there are these two kinds of evil in the soul — the one which is generally called vice, and is obviously a disease of the soul...","st":"228e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"228e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there is the other, which they call ignorance, and which, because existing only in the soul, they will not allow to be vice.","st":"228e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I certainly admit what I at first disputed — that there are two kinds of vice in the soul, and that we ought to consider cowardice, intemperance, and injustice to be alike forms of disease in the soul, and ignorance, of which there are all sorts of varieties, to be deformity.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And in the case of the body are there not two arts which have to do with the two bodily states?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is gymnastic, which has to do with deformity, and medicine, which has to do with disease.","st":"229b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"229b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And where there is insolence and injustice and cowardice, is not chastisement the art which is most required?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That certainly appears to be the opinion of mankind.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Again, of the various kinds of ignorance, may not instruction be rightly said to be the remedy?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And of the art of instruction, shall we say that there is one or many kinds? At any rate there are two principal ones. Think.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I will.","st":"229c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I believe that I can see how we shall soonest arrive at the answer to this question.","st":"229c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How?","st":"229c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> If we can discover a line which divides ignorance into two halves. For a division of ignorance into two parts will certainly imply that the art of instruction is also twofold, answering to the two divisions of ignorance.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Well, and do you see what you are looking for?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I do seem to myself to see one very large and bad sort of ignorance which is quite separate, and may be weighed in the scale against all other sorts of ignorance put together.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When a person supposes that he knows, and does not know; this appears to be the great source of all the errors of the intellect.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And this, if I am not mistaken, is the kind of ignorance which specially earns the title of stupidity.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> What name, then, shall be given to the sort of instruction which gets rid of this?","st":"229e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The instruction which you mean, Stranger, is, I should imagine, not the teaching of handicraft arts, but what, thanks to us, has been termed education in this part the world.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, Theaetetus, and by nearly all Hellenes. But we have still to consider whether education admits of any further division.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> We have.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I think that there is a point at which such a division is possible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Where?","st":"230a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Of education, one method appears to be rougher, and another smoother.","st":"230a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How are we to distinguish the two?","st":"230a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is the time-honoured mode which our fathers commonly practised towards their sons, and which is still adopted by many — either of roughly reproving their errors, or of gently advising them; which varieties may be correctly included under the general term of admonition.","st":"230a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"230b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But whereas some appear to have arrived at the conclusion that all ignorance is involuntary, and that no one who thinks himself wise is willing to learn any of those things in which he is conscious of his own cleverness, and that the admonitory sort of instruction gives much trouble and does little good —","st":"230b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There they are quite right.","st":"230b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Accordingly, they set to work to eradicate the spirit of conceit in another way.","st":"230b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> In what way?","st":"230b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> They cross-examine a man’s words, when he thinks that he is saying something and is really saying nothing, and easily convict him of inconsistencies in his opinions; these they then collect by the dialectical process, and placing them side by side, show that they contradict one another about the same things, in relation to the same things, and in the same respect. He, seeing this, is angry with himself, and grows gentle towards others, and thus is entirely delivered from great prejudices and harsh notions, in a way which is most amusing to the hearer, and produces the most lasting good effect on the person who is the subject of the operation. For as the physician considers that the body will receive no benefit from taking food until the internal obstacles have been removed, so the purifier of the soul is conscious that his patient will receive no benefit from the application of knowledge until he is refuted, and from refutation learns modesty; he must be purged of his prejudices first and made to think that he knows only what he knows, and no more.","st":"230b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is certainly the best and wisest state of mind.","st":"230e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> For all these reasons, Theaetetus, we must admit that refutation is the greatest and chiefest of purifications, and he who has not been refuted, though he be the Great King himself, is in an awful state of impurity; he is uninstructed and deformed in those things in which he who would be truly blessed ought to be fairest and purest.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And who are the ministers of this art? I am afraid to say the Sophists.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Lest we should assign to them too high a prerogative.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yet the Sophist has a certain likeness to our minister of purification.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, the same sort of likeness which a wolf, who is the fiercest of animals, has to a dog, who is the gentlest. But he who would not be found tripping, ought to be very careful in this matter of comparisons, for they are most slippery things. Nevertheless, let us assume that the Sophists are the men. I say this provisionally, for I think that the line which divides them will be marked enough if proper care is taken.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Likely enough.","st":"231c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us grant, then, that from the discerning art comes purification, and from purification let there be separated off a part which is concerned with the soul; of this mental purification instruction is a portion, and of instruction education, and of education, that refutation of vain conceit which has been discovered in the present argument; and let this be called by you and me the nobly-descended art of Sophistry.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very well; and yet, considering the number of forms in which he has presented himself, I begin to doubt how I can with any truth or confidence describe the real nature of the Sophist.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You naturally feel perplexed; and yet I think that he must be still more perplexed in his attempt to escape us, for as the proverb says, when every way is blocked, there is no escape; now, then, is the time of all others to set upon him.","st":"231d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> First let us wait a moment and recover breath, and while we are resting, we may reckon up in how many forms he has appeared. In the first place, he was discovered to be a paid hunter after wealth and youth.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"231e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In the second place, he was a merchant in the goods of the soul.","st":"231e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In the third place, he has turned out to be a retailer of the same sort of wares.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; and in the fourth place, he himself manufactured the learned wares which he sold.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Quite right; I will try and remember the fifth myself. He belonged to the fighting class, and was further distinguished as a hero of debate, who professed the eristic art.","st":"232a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"232a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The sixth point was doubtful, and yet we at last agreed that he was a purger of souls, who cleared away notions obstructive to knowledge.","st":"232a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"232a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you not see that when the professor of any art has one name and many kinds of knowledge, there must be something wrong? The multiplicity of names which is applied to him shows that the common principle to which all these branches of knowledge are tending, is not understood.","st":"232b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I should imagine this to be the case.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> At any rate we will understand him, and no indolence shall prevent us. Let us begin again, then, and re-examine some of our statements concerning the Sophist; there was one thing which appeared to me especially characteristic of him.","st":"232c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To what are you referring?","st":"232c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We were saying of him, if I am not mistaken, that he was a disputer?","st":"232c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> We were.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And does he not also teach others the art of disputation?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly he does.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And about what does he profess that he teaches men to dispute? To begin at the beginning — Does he make them able to dispute about divine things, which are invisible to men in general?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> At any rate, he is said to do so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what do you say of the visible things in heaven and earth, and the like?","st":"232d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly he disputes, and teaches to dispute about them.","st":"232d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, again, in private conversation, when any universal assertion is made about generation and essence, we know that such persons are tremendous argufiers, and are able to impart their own skill to others.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Undoubtedly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And do they not profess to make men able to dispute about law and about politics in general?","st":"232e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why, no one would have anything to say to them, if they did not make these professions.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In all and every art, what the craftsman ought to say in answer to any question is written down in a popular form, and he who likes may learn.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I suppose that you are referring to the precepts of Protagoras about wrestling and the other arts?","st":"233a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, my friend, and about a good many other things. In a word, is not the art of disputation a power of disputing about all things?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly; there does not seem to be much which is left out.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But oh! my dear youth, do you suppose this possible? for perhaps your young eyes may see things which to our duller sight do not appear.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To what are you alluding? I do not think that I understand your present question.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I ask whether anybody can understand all things.","st":"233b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Happy would mankind be if such a thing were possible!","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But how can any one who is ignorant dispute in a rational manner against him who knows?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> He cannot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then why has the sophistical art such a mysterious power?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To what do you refer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> How do the Sophists make young men believe in their supreme and universal wisdom? For if they neither disputed nor were thought to dispute rightly, or being thought to do so were deemed no wiser for their controversial skill, then, to quote your own observation, no one would give them money or be willing to learn their art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They certainly would not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But they are willing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, they are.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, and the reason, as I should imagine, is that they are supposed to have knowledge of those things about which they dispute?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And they dispute about all things?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And therefore, to their disciples, they appear to be all-wise?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"233d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But they are not; for that was shown to be impossible.","st":"233d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Impossible, of course.","st":"233d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then the Sophist has been shown to have a sort of conjectural or apparent knowledge only of all things, which is not the truth?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Exactly; no better description of him could be given.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us now take an illustration, which will still more clearly explain his nature.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"233e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I will tell you, and you shall answer me, giving your very closest attention. Suppose that a person were to profess, not that he could speak or dispute, but that he knew how to make and do all things, by a single art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> All things?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I see that you do not understand the first word that I utter, for you do not understand the meaning of ‘all.’","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> No, I do not.","st":"234a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Under all things, I include you and me, and also animals and trees.","st":"234a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"234a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Suppose a person to say that he will make you and me, and all creatures.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What would he mean by ‘making’? He cannot be a husbandman; — for you said that he is a maker of animals.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes; and I say that he is also the maker of the sea, and the earth, and the heavens, and the gods, and of all other things; and, further, that he can make them in no time, and sell them for a few pence.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That must be a jest.","st":"234b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And when a man says that he knows all things, and can teach them to another at a small cost, and in a short time, is not that a jest?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is there any more artistic or graceful form of jest than imitation?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not; and imitation is a very comprehensive term, which includes under one class the most diverse sorts of things.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We know, of course, that he who professes by one art to make all things is really a painter, and by the painter’s art makes resemblances of real things which have the same name with them; and he can deceive the less intelligent sort of young children, to whom he shows his pictures at a distance, into the belief that he has the absolute power of making whatever he likes.","st":"234c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And may there not be supposed to be an imitative art of reasoning? Is it not possible to enchant the hearts of young men by words poured through their ears, when they are still at a distance from the truth of facts, by exhibiting to them fictitious arguments, and making them think that they are true, and that the speaker is the wisest of men in all things?","st":"234d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; why should there not be another such art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But as time goes on, and their hearers advance in years, and come into closer contact with realities, and have learnt by sad experience to see and feel the truth of things, are not the greater part of them compelled to change many opinions which they formerly entertained, so that the great appears small to them, and the easy difficult, and all their dreamy speculations are overturned by the facts of life?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is my view, as far as I can judge, although, at my age, I may be one of those who see things at a distance only.","st":"234e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the wish of all of us, who are your friends, is and always will be to bring you as near to the truth as we can without the sad reality. And now I should like you to tell me, whether the Sophist is not visibly a magician and imitator of true being; or are we still disposed to think that he may have a true knowledge of the various matters about which he disputes?","st":"234e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> But how can he, Stranger? Is there any doubt, after what has been said, that he is to be located in one of the divisions of children’s play?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we must place him in the class of magicians and mimics.","st":"235a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly we must.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now our business is not to let the animal out, for we have got him in a sort of dialectical net, and there is one thing which he decidedly will not escape.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The inference that he is a juggler.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Precisely my own opinion of him.","st":"235b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, clearly, we ought as soon as possible to divide the image-making art, and go down into the net, and, if the Sophist does not run away from us, to seize him according to orders and deliver him over to reason, who is the lord of the hunt, and proclaim the capture of him; and if he creeps into the recesses of the imitative art, and secretes himself in one of them, to divide again and follow him up until in some sub-section of imitation he is caught. For our method of tackling each and all is one which neither he nor any other creature will ever escape in triumph.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Well said; and let us do as you propose.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Well, then, pursuing the same analytic method as before, I think that I can discern two divisions of the imitative art, but I am not as yet able to see in which of them the desired form is to be found.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Will you tell me first what are the two divisions of which you are speaking?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> One is the art of likeness-making; — generally a likeness of anything is made by producing a copy which is executed according to the proportions of the original, similar in length and breadth and depth, each thing receiving also its appropriate colour.","st":"235d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Is not this always the aim of imitation?","st":"235d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Not always; in works either of sculpture or of painting, which are of any magnitude, there is a certain degree of deception; for artists were to give the true proportions of their fair works, the upper part, which is farther off, would appear to be out of proportion in comparison with the lower, which is nearer; and so they give up the truth in their images and make only the proportions which appear to be beautiful, disregarding the real ones.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And that which being other is also like, may we not fairly call a likeness or image?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"236a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And may we not, as I did just now, call that part of the imitative art which is concerned with making such images the art of likeness-making?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Let that be the name.","st":"236b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what shall we call those resemblances of the beautiful, which appear such owing to the unfavourable position of the spectator, whereas if a person had the power of getting a correct view of works of such magnitude, they would appear not even like that to which they profess to be like? May we not call these ‘appearances,’ since they appear only and are not really like?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is a great deal of this kind of thing in painting, and in all imitation.","st":"236c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And may we not fairly call the sort of art, which produces an appearance and not an image, phantastic art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most fairly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> These then are the two kinds of image-making — the art of making likenesses, and phantastic or the art of making appearances?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"236d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I was doubtful before in which of them I should place the Sophist, nor am I even now able to see clearly; verily he is a wonderful and inscrutable creature. And now in the cleverest manner he has got into an impossible place.","st":"236d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, he has.","st":"236d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you speak advisedly, or are you carried away at the moment by the habit of assenting into giving a hasty answer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> May I ask to what you are referring?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> My dear friend, we are engaged in a very difficult speculation — there can be no doubt of that; for how a thing can appear and seem, and not be, or how a man can say a thing which is not true, has always been and still remains a very perplexing question. Can any one say or think that falsehood really exists, and avoid being caught in a contradiction? Indeed, Theaetetus, the task is a difficult one.","st":"236e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why?","st":"236e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> He who says that falsehood exists has the audacity to assert the being of not-being; for this is implied in the possibility of falsehood. But, my boy, in the days when I was a boy, the great Parmenides protested against this doctrine, and to the end of his life he continued to inculcate the same lesson — always repeating both in verse and out of verse:","st":"237a"},{"sp":"N","t":"‘Keep your mind from this way of enquiry, for never will you show that not-being is.’","st":"237a"},{"sp":"N","t":"Such is his testimony, which is confirmed by the very expression when sifted a little. Would you object to begin with the consideration of the words themselves?","st":"237a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Never mind about me; I am only desirous that you should carry on the argument in the best way, and that you should take me with you.","st":"237a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Very good; and now say, do we venture to utter the forbidden word ‘not-being’?","st":"237b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly we do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us be serious then, and consider the question neither in strife nor play: suppose that one of the hearers of Parmenides was asked, ‘To what is the term “not-being” to be applied?’ — do you know what sort of object he would single out in reply, and what answer he would make to the enquirer?","st":"237c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is a difficult question, and one not to be answered at all by a person like myself.","st":"237c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is at any rate no difficulty in seeing that the predicate ‘not-being’ is not applicable to any being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> None, certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And if not to being, then not to something.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> It is also plain, that in speaking of something we speak of being, for to speak of an abstract something naked and isolated from all being is impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Impossible.","st":"237d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You mean by assenting to imply that he who says something must say some one thing?","st":"237d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"237d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Some in the singular (ti) you would say is the sign of one, some in the dual (tine) of two, some in the plural (tines) of many?","st":"237d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Exactly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then he who says ‘not something’ must say absolutely nothing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most assuredly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And as we cannot admit that a man speaks and says nothing, he who says ‘not-being’ does not speak at all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The difficulty of the argument can no further go.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Not yet, my friend, is the time for such a word; for there still remains of all perplexities the first and greatest, touching the very foundation of the matter.","st":"237e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean? Do not be afraid to speak.","st":"237e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> To that which is, may be attributed some other thing which is?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But can anything which is, be attributed to that which is not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And all number is to be reckoned among things which are?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, surely number, if anything, has a real existence.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we must not attempt to attribute to not-being number either in the singular or plural?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The argument implies that we should be wrong in doing so.","st":"238a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But how can a man either express in words or even conceive in thought things which are not or a thing which is not without number?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How indeed?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When we speak of things which are not, are we not attributing plurality to not-being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"238b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But, on the other hand, when we say ‘what is not,’ do we not attribute unity?","st":"238b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Manifestly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Nevertheless, we maintain that you may not and ought not to attribute being to not-being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you see, then, that not-being in itself can neither be spoken, uttered, or thought, but that it is unthinkable, unutterable, unspeakable, indescribable?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But, if so, I was wrong in telling you just now that the difficulty which was coming is the greatest of all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What! is there a greater still behind?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Well, I am surprised, after what has been said already, that you do not see the difficulty in which he who would refute the notion of not-being is involved. For he is compelled to contradict himself as soon as he makes the attempt.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean? Speak more clearly.","st":"238d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do not expect clearness from me. For I, who maintain that not-being has no part either in the one or many, just now spoke and am still speaking of not-being as one; for I say ‘not-being.’ Do you understand?","st":"238d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And a little while ago I said that not-being is unutterable, unspeakable, indescribable: do you follow?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I do after a fashion.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When I introduced the word ’is,’ did I not contradict what I said before?","st":"238e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"238e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And in using the singular verb, did I not speak of not-being as one?","st":"238e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"238e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And when I spoke of not-being as indescribable and unspeakable and unutterable, in using each of these words in the singular, did I not refer to not-being as one?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet we say that, strictly speaking, it should not be defined as one or many, and should not even be called ‘it,’ for the use of the word ‘it’ would imply a form of unity.","st":"239a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> How, then, can any one put any faith in me? For now, as always, I am unequal to the refutation of not-being. And therefore, as I was saying, do not look to me for the right way of speaking about not-being; but come, let us try the experiment with you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Make a noble effort, as becomes youth, and endeavour with all your might to speak of not-being in a right manner, without introducing into it either existence or unity or plurality.","st":"239b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> It would be a strange boldness in me which would attempt the task when I see you thus discomfited.","st":"239b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Say no more of ourselves; but until we find some one or other who can speak of not-being without number, we must acknowledge that the Sophist is a clever rogue who will not be got out of his hole.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And if we say to him that he professes an art of making appearances, he will grapple with us and retort our argument upon ourselves; and when we call him an image-maker he will say, ‘Pray what do you mean at all by an image?’ — and I should like to know, Theaetetus, how we can possibly answer the younker’s question?","st":"239c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> We shall doubtless tell him of the images which are reflected in water or in mirrors; also of sculptures, pictures, and other duplicates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I see, Theaetetus, that you have never made the acquaintance of the Sophist.","st":"239d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why do you think so?","st":"239d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> He will make believe to have his eyes shut, or to have none.","st":"239d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"239d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When you tell him of something existing in a mirror, or in sculpture, and address him as though he had eyes, he will laugh you to scorn, and will pretend that he knows nothing of mirrors and streams, or of sight at all; he will say that he is asking about an idea.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What can he mean?","st":"239e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The common notion pervading all these objects, which you speak of as many, and yet call by the single name of image, as though it were the unity under which they were all included. How will you maintain your ground against him?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How, Stranger, can I describe an image except as something fashioned in the likeness of the true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And do you mean this something to be some other true thing, or what do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not another true thing, but only a resemblance.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And you mean by true that which really is?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the not true is that which is the opposite of the true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Exactly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> A resemblance, then, is not really real, if, as you say, not true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Nay, but it is in a certain sense.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You mean to say, not in a true sense?","st":"240b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; it is in reality only an image.","st":"240b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then what we call an image is in reality really unreal.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> In what a strange complication of being and not-being we are involved!","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Strange! I should think so. See how, by his reciprocation of opposites, the many-headed Sophist has compelled us, quite against our will, to admit the existence of not-being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, indeed, I see.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The difficulty is how to define his art without falling into a contradiction.","st":"240c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How do you mean? And where does the danger lie?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When we say that he deceives us with an illusion, and that his art is illusory, do we mean that our soul is led by his art to think falsely, or what do we mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There is nothing else to be said.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Again, false opinion is that form of opinion which thinks the opposite of the truth: — You would assent?","st":"240d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You mean to say that false opinion thinks what is not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Does false opinion think that things which are not are not, or that in a certain sense they are?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Things that are not must be imagined to exist in a certain sense, if any degree of falsehood is to be possible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And does not false opinion also think that things which most certainly exist do not exist at all?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And here, again, is falsehood?","st":"240e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Falsehood — yes.","st":"240e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And in like manner, a false proposition will be deemed to be one which asserts the non-existence of things which are, and the existence of things which are not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There is no other way in which a false proposition can arise.","st":"241a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is not; but the Sophist will deny these statements. And indeed how can any rational man assent to them, when the very expressions which we have just used were before acknowledged by us to be unutterable, unspeakable, indescribable, unthinkable? Do you see his point, Theaetetus?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course he will say that we are contradicting ourselves when we hazard the assertion, that falsehood exists in opinion and in words; for in maintaining this, we are compelled over and over again to assert being of not-being, which we admitted just now to be an utter impossibility.","st":"241b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> How well you remember! And now it is high time to hold a consultation as to what we ought to do about the Sophist; for if we persist in looking for him in the class of false workers and magicians, you see that the handles for objection and the difficulties which will arise are very numerous and obvious.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They are indeed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We have gone through but a very small portion of them, and they are really infinite.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> If that is the case, we cannot possibly catch the Sophist.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Shall we then be so faint-hearted as to give him up?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not, I should say, if we can get the slightest hold upon him.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Will you then forgive me, and, as your words imply, not be altogether displeased if I flinch a little from the grasp of such a sturdy argument?","st":"241c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure I will.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I have a yet more urgent request to make.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Which is — ?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> That you will promise not to regard me as a parricide.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And why?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Because, in self-defence, I must test the philosophy of my father Parmenides, and try to prove by main force that in a certain sense not-being is, and that being, on the other hand, is not.","st":"241d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Some attempt of the kind is clearly needed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, a blind man, as they say, might see that, and, unless these questions are decided in one way or another, no one when he speaks of false words, or false opinion, or idols, or images, or imitations, or appearances, or about the arts which are concerned with them; can avoid falling into ridiculous contradictions.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"241e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And therefore I must venture to lay hands on my father’s argument; for if I am to be over-scrupulous, I shall have to give the matter up.","st":"241e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Nothing in the world should ever induce us to do so.","st":"241e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I have a third little request which I wish to make.","st":"241e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"241e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You heard me say what I have always felt and still feel — that I have no heart for this argument?","st":"242a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I did.","st":"242a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I tremble at the thought of what I have said, and expect that you will deem me mad, when you hear of my sudden changes and shiftings; let me therefore observe, that I am examining the question entirely out of regard for you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There is no reason for you to fear that I shall impute any impropriety to you, if you attempt this refutation and proof; take heart, therefore, and proceed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And where shall I begin the perilous enterprise? I think that the road which I must take is —","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Which? — Let me hear.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I think that we had better, first of all, consider the points which at present are regarded as self-evident, lest we may have fallen into some confusion, and be too ready to assent to one another, fancying that we are quite clear about them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Say more distinctly what you mean.","st":"242c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I think that Parmenides, and all ever yet undertook to determine the number and nature of existences, talked to us in rather a light and easy strain.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> As if we had been children, to whom they repeated each his own mythus or story; — one said that there were three principles, and that at one time there was war between certain of them; and then again there was peace, and they were married and begat children, and brought them up; and another spoke of two principles, — a moist and a dry, or a hot and a cold, and made them marry and cohabit. The Eleatics, however, in our part of the world, say that all things are many in name, but in nature one; this is their mythus, which goes back to Xenophanes, and is even older. Then there are Ionian, and in more recent times Sicilian muses, who have arrived at the conclusion that to unite the two principles is safer, and to say that being is one and many, and that these are held together by enmity and friendship, ever parting, ever meeting, as the severer Muses assert, while the gentler ones do not insist on the perpetual strife and peace, but admit a relaxation and alternation of them; peace and unity sometimes prevailing under the sway of Aphrodite, and then again plurality and war, by reason of a principle of strife. Whether any of them spoke the truth in all this is hard to determine; besides, antiquity and famous men should have reverence, and not be liable to accusations so serious. Yet one thing may be said of them without offence —","st":"242d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What thing?","st":"242d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> That they went on their several ways disdaining to notice people like ourselves; they did not care whether they took us with them, or left us behind them.","st":"242d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How do you mean?","st":"242d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I mean to say, that when they talk of one, two, or more elements, which are or have become or are becoming, or again of heat mingling with cold, assuming in some other part of their works separations and mixtures, — tell me, Theaetetus, do you understand what they mean by these expressions? When I was a younger man, I used to fancy that I understood quite well what was meant by the term ‘not-being,’ which is our present subject of dispute; and now you see in what a fix we are about it.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I see.","st":"243a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And very likely we have been getting into the same perplexity about ‘being,’ and yet may fancy that when anybody utters the word, we understand him quite easily, although we do not know about not-being. But we may be; equally ignorant of both.","st":"243a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I dare say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the same may be said of all the terms just mentioned.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The consideration of most of them may be deferred; but we had better now discuss the chief captain and leader of them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of what are you speaking? You clearly think that we must first investigate what people mean by the word ‘being.’","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You follow close at my heels, Theaetetus. For the right method, I conceive, will be to call into our presence the dualistic philosophers and to interrogate them. ‘Come,’ we will say, ‘Ye, who affirm that hot and cold or any other two principles are the universe, what is this term which you apply to both of them, and what do you mean when you say that both and each of them “are”? How are we to understand the word “are”? Upon your view, are we to suppose that there is a third principle over and above the other two, — three in all, and not two? For clearly you cannot say that one of the two principles is being, and yet attribute being equally to both of them; for, if you did, whichever of the two is identified with being, will comprehend the other; and so they will be one and not two.’","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But perhaps you mean to give the name of ‘being’ to both of them together?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite likely.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> ‘Then, friends,’ we shall reply to them, ‘the answer is plainly that the two will still be resolved into one.’","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> ‘Since, then, we are in a difficulty, please to tell us what you mean, when you speak of being; for there can be no doubt that you always from the first understood your own meaning, whereas we once thought that we understood you, but now we are in a great strait. Please to begin by explaining this matter to us, and let us no longer fancy that we understand you, when we entirely misunderstand you.’ There will be no impropriety in our demanding an answer to this question, either of the dualists or of the pluralists?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what about the assertors of the oneness of the all — must we not endeavour to ascertain from them what they mean by ‘being’?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> By all means.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then let them answer this question: One, you say, alone is? ‘Yes,’ they will reply.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there is something which you call ‘being’?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> ‘Yes.’","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is being the same as one, and do you apply two names to the same thing?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What will be their answer, Stranger?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> It is clear, Theaetetus, that he who asserts the unity of being will find a difficulty in answering this or any other question.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why so?","st":"244c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> To admit of two names, and to affirm that there is nothing but unity, is surely ridiculous?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And equally irrational to admit that a name is anything?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> To distinguish the name from the thing, implies duality.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"244d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet he who identifies the name with the thing will be compelled to say that it is the name of nothing, or if he says that it is the name of something, even then the name will only be the name of a name, and of nothing else.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the one will turn out to be only one of one, and being absolute unity, will represent a mere name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And would they say that the whole is other than the one that is, or the same with it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure they would, and they actually say so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> If being is a whole, as Parmenides sings, —","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"‘Every way like unto the fullness of a well-rounded sphere, Evenly balanced from the centre on every side, And must needs be neither greater nor less in any way, Neither on this side nor on that—’","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"then being has a centre and extremes, and, having these, must also have parts.","st":"244e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yet that which has parts may have the attribute of unity in all the parts, and in this way being all and a whole, may be one?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But that of which this is the condition cannot be absolute unity?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Because, according to right reason, that which is truly one must be affirmed to be absolutely indivisible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"245a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But this indivisible, if made up of many parts, will contradict reason.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I understand.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Shall we say that being is one and a whole, because it has the attribute of unity? Or shall we say that being is not a whole at all?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is a hard alternative to offer.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Most true; for being, having in a certain sense the attribute of one, is yet proved not to be the same as one, and the all is therefore more than one.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"245b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet if being be not a whole, through having the attribute of unity, and there be such a thing as an absolute whole, being lacks something of its own nature?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Upon this view, again, being, having a defect of being, will become not-being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, again, the all becomes more than one, for being and the whole will each have their separate nature.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But if the whole does not exist at all, all the previous difficulties remain the same, and there will be the further difficulty, that besides having no being, being can never have come into being.","st":"245c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Because that which comes into being always comes into being as a whole, so that he who does not give whole a place among beings, cannot speak either of essence or generation as existing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, that certainly appears to be true.","st":"245d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Again; how can that which is not a whole have any quantity? For that which is of a certain quantity must necessarily be the whole of that quantity.","st":"245d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Exactly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there will be innumerable other points, each of them causing infinite trouble to him who says that being is either one or two.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The difficulties which are dawning upon us prove this; for one objection connects with another, and they are always involving what has preceded in a greater and worse perplexity.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We are far from having exhausted the more exact thinkers who treat of being and not-being. But let us be content to leave them, and proceed to view those who speak less precisely; and we shall find as the result of all, that the nature of being is quite as difficult to comprehend as that of not-being.","st":"245e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Then now we will go to the others.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There appears to be a sort of war of Giants and Gods going on amongst them; they are fighting with one another about the nature of essence.","st":"246a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How is that?","st":"246a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Some of them are dragging down all things from heaven and from the unseen to earth, and they literally grasp in their hands rocks and oaks; of these they lay hold, and obstinately maintain, that the things only which can be touched or handled have being or essence, because they define being and body as one, and if any one else says that what is not a body exists they altogether despise him, and will hear of nothing but body.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I have often met with such men, and terrible fellows they are.","st":"246b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And that is the reason why their opponents cautiously defend themselves from above, out of an unseen world, mightily contending that true essence consists of certain intelligible and incorporeal ideas; the bodies of the materialists, which by them are maintained to be the very truth, they break up into little bits by their arguments, and affirm them to be, not essence, but generation and motion. Between the two armies, Theaetetus, there is always an endless conflict raging concerning these matters.","st":"246b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"246c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us ask each party in turn, to give an account of that which they call essence.","st":"246c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How shall we get it out of them?","st":"246c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> With those who make being to consist in ideas, there will be less difficulty, for they are civil people enough; but there will be very great difficulty, or rather an absolute impossibility, in getting an opinion out of those who drag everything down to matter. Shall I tell you what we must do?","st":"246c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us, if we can, really improve them; but if this is not possible, let us imagine them to be better than they are, and more willing to answer in accordance with the rules of argument, and then their opinion will be more worth having; for that which better men acknowledge has more weight than that which is acknowledged by inferior men. Moreover we are no respecters of persons, but seekers after truth.","st":"246d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then now, on the supposition that they are improved, let us ask them to state their views, and do you interpret them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Agreed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let them say whether they would admit that there is such a thing as a mortal animal.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course they would.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And do they not acknowledge this to be a body having a soul?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly they do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Meaning to say that the soul is something which exists?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And do they not say that one soul is just, and another unjust, and that one soul is wise, and another foolish?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"247a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And that the just and wise soul becomes just and wise by the possession of justice and wisdom, and the opposite under opposite circumstances?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, they do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But surely that which may be present or may be absent will be admitted by them to exist?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, allowing that justice, wisdom, the other virtues, and their opposites exist, as well as a soul in which they inhere, do they affirm any of them to be visible and tangible, or are they all invisible?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They would say that hardly any of them are visible.","st":"247b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And would they say that they are corporeal?","st":"247b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They would distinguish: the soul would be said by them to have a body; but as to the other qualities of justice, wisdom, and the like, about which you asked, they would not venture either to deny their existence, or to maintain that they were all corporeal.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Verily, Theaetetus, I perceive a great improvement in them; the real aborigines, children of the dragon’s teeth, would have been deterred by no shame at all, but would have obstinately asserted that nothing is which they are not able to squeeze in their hands.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is pretty much their notion.","st":"247c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us push the question; for if they will admit that any, even the smallest particle of being, is incorporeal, it is enough; they must then say what that nature is which is common to both the corporeal and incorporeal, and which they have in their mind’s eye when they say of both of them that they ‘are.’ Perhaps they may be in a difficulty; and if this is the case, there is a possibility that they may accept a notion of ours respecting the nature of being, having nothing of their own to offer.","st":"247d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is the notion? Tell me, and we shall soon see.","st":"247d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> My notion would be, that anything which possesses any sort of power to affect another, or to be affected by another, if only for a single moment, however trifling the cause and however slight the effect, has real existence; and I hold that the definition of being is simply power.","st":"247d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They accept your suggestion, having nothing better of their own to offer.","st":"247e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Very good; perhaps we, as well as they, may one day change our minds; but, for the present, this may be regarded as the understanding which is established with them.","st":"247e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Agreed.","st":"247e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us now go to the friends of ideas; of their opinions, too, you shall be the interpreter.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I will.","st":"248a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> To them we say — You would distinguish essence from generation?","st":"248a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> ‘Yes,’ they reply.","st":"248a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And you would allow that we participate in generation with the body, and through perception, but we participate with the soul through thought in true essence; and essence you would affirm to be always the same and immutable, whereas generation or becoming varies?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; that is what we should affirm.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Well, fair sirs, we say to them, what is this participation, which you assert of both? Do you agree with our recent definition?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What definition?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We said that being was an active or passive energy, arising out of a certain power which proceeds from elements meeting with one another. Perhaps your ears, Theaetetus, may fail to catch their answer, which I recognize because I have been accustomed to hear it.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And what is their answer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> They deny the truth of what we were just now saying to the aborigines about existence.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What was that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Any power of doing or suffering in a degree however slight was held by us to be a sufficient definition of being?","st":"248c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"248c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> They deny this, and say that the power of doing or suffering is confined to becoming, and that neither power is applicable to being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And is there not some truth in what they say?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes; but our reply will be, that we want to ascertain from them more distinctly, whether they further admit that the soul knows, and that being or essence is known.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There can be no doubt that they say so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is knowing and being known doing or suffering, or both, or is the one doing and the other suffering, or has neither any share in either?","st":"248d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Clearly, neither has any share in either; for if they say anything else, they will contradict themselves.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I understand; but they will allow that if to know is active, then, of course, to be known is passive. And on this view being, in so far as it is known, is acted upon by knowledge, and is therefore in motion; for that which is in a state of rest cannot be acted upon, as we affirm.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"248e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, O heavens, can we ever be made to believe that motion and life and soul and mind are not present with perfect being? Can we imagine that being is devoid of life and mind, and exists in awful unmeaningness an everlasting fixture?","st":"248e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That would be a dreadful thing to admit, Stranger.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But shall we say that has mind and not life?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How is that possible?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Or shall we say that both inhere in perfect being, but that it has no soul which contains them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And in what other way can it contain them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Or that being has mind and life and soul, but although endowed with soul remains absolutely unmoved?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> All three suppositions appear to me to be irrational.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Under being, then, we must include motion, and that which is moved.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"249b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, Theaetetus, our inference is, that if there is no motion, neither is there any mind anywhere, or about anything or belonging to any one.","st":"249b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet this equally follows, if we grant that all things are in motion — upon this view too mind has no existence.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you think that sameness of condition and mode and subject could ever exist without a principle of rest?","st":"249c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"249c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Can you see how without them mind could exist, or come into existence anywhere?","st":"249c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And surely contend we must in every possible way against him who would annihilate knowledge and reason and mind, and yet ventures to speak confidently about anything.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, with all our might.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then the philosopher, who has the truest reverence for these qualities, cannot possibly accept the notion of those who say that the whole is at rest, either as unity or in many forms: and he will be utterly deaf to those who assert universal motion. As children say entreatingly ‘Give us both,’ so he will include both the moveable and immoveable in his definition of being and all.","st":"249d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now, do we seem to have gained a fair notion of being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes truly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Alas, Theaetetus, methinks that we are now only beginning to see the real difficulty of the enquiry into the nature of it.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> O my friend, do you not see that nothing can exceed our ignorance, and yet we fancy that we are saying something good?","st":"249e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I certainly thought that we were; and I do not at all understand how we never found out our desperate case.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Reflect: after having made these admissions, may we not be justly asked the same questions which we ourselves were asking of those who said that all was hot and cold?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What were they? Will you recall them to my mind?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> To be sure I will, and I will remind you of them, by putting the same questions to you which I did to them, and then we shall get on.","st":"250a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"250a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Would you not say that rest and motion are in the most entire opposition to one another?","st":"250a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"250a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet you would say that both and either of them equally are?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I should.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And when you admit that both or either of them are, do you mean to say that both or either of them are in motion?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Or do you wish to imply that they are both at rest, when you say that they are?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course not.","st":"250b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then you conceive of being as some third and distinct nature, under which rest and motion are alike included; and, observing that they both participate in being, you declare that they are.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Truly we seem to have an intimation that being is some third thing, when we say that rest and motion are.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then being is not the combination of rest and motion, but something different from them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> So it would appear.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Being, then, according to its own nature, is neither in motion nor at rest.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is very much the truth.","st":"250c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Where, then, is a man to look for help who would have any clear or fixed notion of being in his mind?","st":"250c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Where, indeed?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I scarcely think that he can look anywhere; for that which is not in motion must be at rest, and again, that which is not at rest must be in motion; but being is placed outside of both these classes. Is this possible?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Utterly impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Here, then, is another thing which we ought to bear in mind.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What?","st":"250d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When we were asked to what we were to assign the appellation of not-being, we were in the greatest difficulty: — do you remember?","st":"250d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"250d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And are we not now in as great a difficulty about being?","st":"250d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I should say, Stranger, that we are in one which is, if possible, even greater.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then let us acknowledge the difficulty; and as being and not-being are involved in the same perplexity, there is hope that when the one appears more or less distinctly, the other will equally appear; and if we are able to see neither, there may still be a chance of steering our way in between them, without any great discredit.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us enquire, then, how we come to predicate many names of the same thing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Give an example.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I mean that we speak of man, for example, under many names — that we attribute to him colours and forms and magnitudes and virtues and vices, in all of which instances and in ten thousand others we not only speak of him as a man, but also as good, and having numberless other attributes, and in the same way anything else which we originally supposed to be one is described by us as many, and under many names.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And thus we provide a rich feast for tyros, whether young or old; for there is nothing easier than to argue that the one cannot be many, or the many one; and great is their delight in denying that a man is good; for man, they insist, is man and good is good. I dare say that you have met with persons who take an interest in such matters — they are often elderly men, whose meagre sense is thrown into amazement by these discoveries of theirs, which they believe to be the height of wisdom.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly, I have.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, not to exclude any one who has ever speculated at all upon the nature of being, let us put our questions to them as well as to our former friends.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What questions?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Shall we refuse to attribute being to motion and rest, or anything to anything, and assume that they do not mingle, and are incapable of participating in one another? Or shall we gather all into one class of things communicable with one another? Or are some things communicable and others not? — Which of these alternatives, Theaetetus, will they prefer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I have nothing to answer on their behalf. Suppose that you take all these hypotheses in turn, and see what are the consequences which follow from each of them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Very good, and first let us assume them to say that nothing is capable of participating in anything else in any respect; in that case rest and motion cannot participate in being at all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> They cannot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But would either of them be if not participating in being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then by this admission everything is instantly overturned, as well the doctrine of universal motion as of universal rest, and also the doctrine of those who distribute being into immutable and everlasting kinds; for all these add on a notion of being, some affirming that things ‘are’ truly in motion, and others that they ‘are’ truly at rest.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Just so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Again, those who would at one time compound, and at another resolve all things, whether making them into one and out of one creating infinity, or dividing them into finite elements, and forming compounds out of these; whether they suppose the processes of creation to be successive or continuous, would be talking nonsense in all this if there were no admixture.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Most ridiculous of all will the men themselves be who want to carry out the argument and yet forbid us to call anything, because participating in some affection from another, by the name of that other.","st":"251e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why so?","st":"251e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Why, because they are compelled to use the words ‘to be,’ ‘apart,’ ‘from others,’ ‘in itself,’ and ten thousand more, which they cannot give up, but must make the connecting links of discourse; and therefore they do not require to be refuted by others, but their enemy, as the saying is, inhabits the same house with them; they are always carrying about with them an adversary, like the wonderful ventriloquist, Eurycles, who out of their own bellies audibly contradicts them.","st":"252a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Precisely so; a very true and exact illustration.","st":"252a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now, if we suppose that all things have the power of communion with one another — what will follow?","st":"252a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Even I can solve that riddle.","st":"252b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> How?","st":"252b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why, because motion itself would be at rest, and rest again in motion, if they could be attributed to one another.","st":"252b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But this is utterly impossible.","st":"252b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then only the third hypothesis remains.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> For, surely, either all things have communion with all; or nothing with any other thing; or some things communicate with some things and others not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And two out of these three suppositions have been found to be impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Every one then, who desires to answer truly, will adopt the third and remaining hypothesis of the communion of some with some.","st":"252d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> This communion of some with some may be illustrated by the case of letters; for some letters do not fit each other, while others do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the vowels, especially, are a sort of bond which pervades all the other letters, so that without a vowel one consonant cannot be joined to another.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But does every one know what letters will unite with what? Or is art required in order to do so?","st":"252e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Art is required.","st":"252e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> What art?","st":"252e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The art of grammar.","st":"252e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is not this also true of sounds high and low? — Is not he who has the art to know what sounds mingle, a musician, and he who is ignorant, not a musician?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"253a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And we shall find this to be generally true of art or the absence of art.","st":"253a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"253a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And as classes are admitted by us in like manner to be some of them capable and others incapable of intermixture, must not he who would rightly show what kinds will unite and what will not, proceed by the help of science in the path of argument? And will he not ask if the connecting links are universal, and so capable of intermixture with all things; and again, in divisions, whether there are not other universal classes, which make them possible?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure he will require science, and, if I am not mistaken, the very greatest of all sciences.","st":"253b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> How are we to call it? By Zeus, have we not lighted unwittingly upon our free and noble science, and in looking for the Sophist have we not entertained the philosopher unawares?","st":"253b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Should we not say that the division according to classes, which neither makes the same other, nor makes other the same, is the business of the dialectical science?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is what we should say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, surely, he who can divide rightly is able to see clearly one form pervading a scattered multitude, and many different forms contained under one higher form; and again, one form knit together into a single whole and pervading many such wholes, and many forms, existing only in separation and isolation. This is the knowledge of classes which determines where they can have communion with one another and where not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"253d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the art of dialectic would be attributed by you only to the philosopher pure and true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Who but he can be worthy?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In this region we shall always discover the philosopher, if we look for him; like the Sophist, he is not easily discovered, but for a different reason.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> For what reason?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Because the Sophist runs away into the darkness of not-being, in which he has learned by habit to feel about, and cannot be discovered because of the darkness of the place. Is not that true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> It seems to be so.","st":"253d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the philosopher, always holding converse through reason with the idea of being, is also dark from excess of light; for the souls of the many have no eye which can endure the vision of the divine.","st":"253d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; that seems to be quite as true as the other.","st":"253d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Well, the philosopher may hereafter be more fully considered by us, if we are disposed; but the Sophist must clearly not be allowed to escape until we have had a good look at him.","st":"253d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"253e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Since, then, we are agreed that some classes have a communion with one another, and others not, and some have communion with a few and others with many, and that there is no reason why some should not have universal communion with all, let us now pursue the enquiry, as the argument suggests, not in relation to all ideas, lest the multitude of them should confuse us, but let us select a few of those which are reckoned to be the principal ones, and consider their several natures and their capacity of communion with one another, in order that if we are not able to apprehend with perfect clearness the notions of being and not-being, we may at least not fall short in the consideration of them, so far as they come within the scope of the present enquiry, if peradventure we may be allowed to assert the reality of not-being, and yet escape unscathed.","st":"254a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> We must do so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The most important of all the genera are those which we were just now mentioning — being and rest and motion.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, by far.","st":"254b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And two of these are, as we affirm, incapable of communion with one another.","st":"254b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite incapable.","st":"254b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Whereas being surely has communion with both of them, for both of them are?","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> That makes up three of them.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And each of them is other than the remaining two, but the same with itself.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But then, what is the meaning of these two words, ‘same’ and ‘other’? Are they two new kinds other than the three, and yet always of necessity intermingling with them, and are we to have five kinds instead of three; or when we speak of the same and other, are we unconsciously speaking of one of the three first kinds?","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very likely we are.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But, surely, motion and rest are neither the other nor the same.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How is that?","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Whatever we attribute to motion and rest in common, cannot be either of them.","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why not?","st":"254c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Because motion would be at rest and rest in motion, for either of them, being predicated of both, will compel the other to change into the opposite of its own nature, because partaking of its opposite.","st":"254d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yet they surely both partake of the same and of the other?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we must not assert that motion, any more than rest, is either the same or the other.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> No; we must not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But are we to conceive that being and the same are identical?","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Possibly.","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But if they are identical, then again in saying that motion and rest have being, we should also be saying that they are the same.","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Which surely cannot be.","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then being and the same cannot be one.","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Scarcely.","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we may suppose the same to be a fourth class, which is now to be added to the three others.","st":"255a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And shall we call the other a fifth class? Or should we consider being and other to be two names of the same class?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very likely.","st":"255b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But you would agree, if I am not mistaken, that existences are relative as well as absolute?","st":"255b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"255b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the other is always relative to other?","st":"255b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"255b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But this would not be the case unless being and the other entirely differed; for, if the other, like being, were absolute as well as relative, then there would have been a kind of other which was not other than other. And now we find that what is other must of necessity be what it is in relation to some other.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is the true state of the case.","st":"255c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we must admit the other as the fifth of our selected classes.","st":"255c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the fifth class pervades all classes, for they all differ from one another, not by reason of their own nature, but because they partake of the idea of the other.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then let us now put the case with reference to each of the five.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> First there is motion, which we affirm to be absolutely ‘other’ than rest: what else can we say?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> It is so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And therefore is not rest.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And yet is, because partaking of being.","st":"255d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"255d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Again, motion is other than the same?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Just so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is therefore not the same.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> It is not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yet, surely, motion is the same, because all things partake of the same.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we must admit, and not object to say, that motion is the same and is not the same, for we do not apply the terms ‘same’ and ‘not the same,’ in the same sense; but we call it the ‘same,’ in relation to itself, because partaking of the same; and not the same, because having communion with the other, it is thereby severed from the same, and has become not that but other, and is therefore rightly spoken of as ‘not the same.’","st":"255e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And if absolute motion in any point of view partook of rest, there would be no absurdity in calling motion stationary.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite right, — that is, on the supposition that some classes mingle with one another, and others not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> That such a communion of kinds is according to nature, we had already proved before we arrived at this part of our discussion.","st":"256a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us proceed, then. May we not say that motion is other than the other, having been also proved by us to be other than the same and other than rest?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is certain.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, according to this view, motion is other and also not other?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> What is the next step? Shall we say that motion is other than the three and not other than the fourth, — for we agreed that there are five classes about and in the sphere of which we proposed to make enquiry?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Surely we cannot admit that the number is less than it appeared to be just now.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we may without fear contend that motion is other than being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Without the least fear.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The plain result is that motion, since it partakes of being, really is and also is not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Nothing can be plainer.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then not-being necessarily exists in the case of motion and of every class; for the nature of the other entering into them all, makes each of them other than being, and so non-existent; and therefore of all of them, in like manner, we may truly say that they are not; and again, inasmuch as they partake of being, that they are and are existent.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> So we may assume.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Every class, then, has plurality of being and infinity of not-being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> So we must infer.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And being itself may be said to be other than the other kinds.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then we may infer that being is not, in respect of as many other things as there are; for not-being these it is itself one, and is not the other things, which are infinite in number.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is not far from the truth.","st":"256d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And we must not quarrel with this result, since it is of the nature of classes to have communion with one another; and if any one denies our present statement [viz., that being is not, etc.], let him first argue with our former conclusion [i.e., respecting the communion of ideas], and then he may proceed to argue with what follows.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Nothing can be fairer.","st":"256e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let me ask you to consider a further question.","st":"256e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What question?","st":"256e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When we speak of not-being, we speak, I suppose, not of something opposed to being, but only different.","st":"256e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"256e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When we speak of something as not great, does the expression seem to you to imply what is little any more than what is equal?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"257a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The negative particles, ou and me, when prefixed to words, do not imply opposition, but only difference from the words, or more correctly from the things represented by the words, which follow them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is another point to be considered, if you do not object.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The nature of the other appears to me to be divided into fractions like knowledge.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Knowledge, like the other, is one; and yet the various parts of knowledge have each of them their own particular name, and hence there are many arts and kinds of knowledge.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And is not the case the same with the parts of the other, which is also one?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very likely; but will you tell me how?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is some part of the other which is opposed to the beautiful?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Shall we say that this has or has not a name?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> It has; for whatever we call not-beautiful is other than the beautiful, not than something else.","st":"257c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now tell me another thing.","st":"257c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What?","st":"257c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Is the not-beautiful anything but this — an existence parted off from a certain kind of existence, and again from another point of view opposed to an existing something?","st":"257c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then the not-beautiful turns out to be the opposition of being to being?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But upon this view, is the beautiful a more real and the not-beautiful a less real existence?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Not at all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the not-great may be said to exist, equally with the great?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, in the same way, the just must be placed in the same category with the not-just — the one cannot be said to have any more existence than the other.","st":"257d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The same may be said of other things; seeing that the nature of the other has a real existence, the parts of this nature must equally be supposed to exist.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, as would appear, the opposition of a part of the other, and of a part of being, to one another, is, if I may venture to say so, as truly essence as being itself, and implies not the opposite of being, but only what is other than being.","st":"257e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Beyond question.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> What then shall we call it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Clearly, not-being; and this is the very nature for which the Sophist compelled us to search.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And has not this, as you were saying, as real an existence as any other class? May I not say with confidence that not-being has an assured existence, and a nature of its own? Just as the great was found to be great and the beautiful beautiful, and the not-great not-great, and the not-beautiful not-beautiful, in the same manner not-being has been found to be and is not-being, and is to be reckoned one among the many classes of being. Do you, Theaetetus, still feel any doubt of this?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> None whatever.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you observe that our scepticism has carried us beyond the range of Parmenides’ prohibition?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> In what?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We have advanced to a further point, and shown him more than he forbad us to investigate.","st":"258b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How is that?","st":"258b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Why, because he says —","st":"258b"},{"sp":"N","t":"‘Not-being never is, and do thou keep thy thoughts from this way of enquiry.’","st":"258b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, he says so.","st":"258b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Whereas, we have not only proved that things which are not are, but we have shown what form of being not-being is; for we have shown that the nature of the other is, and is distributed over all things in their relations to one another, and whatever part of the other is contrasted with being, this is precisely what we have ventured to call not-being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And surely, Stranger, we were quite right.","st":"258c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let not any one say, then, that while affirming the opposition of not-being to being, we still assert the being of not-being; for as to whether there is an opposite of being, to that enquiry we have long said good-bye — it may or may not be, and may or may not be capable of definition. But as touching our present account of not-being, let a man either convince us of error, or, so long as he cannot, he too must say, as we are saying, that there is a communion of classes, and that being, and difference or other, traverse all things and mutually interpenetrate, so that the other partakes of being, and by reason of this participation is, and yet is not that of which it partakes, but other, and being other than being, it is clearly a necessity that not-being should be. And again, being, through partaking of the other, becomes a class other than the remaining classes, and being other than all of them, is not each one of them, and is not all the rest, so that undoubtedly there are thousands upon thousands of cases in which being is not, and all other things, whether regarded individually or collectively, in many respects are, and in many respects are not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And he who is sceptical of this contradiction, must think how he can find something better to say; or if he sees a puzzle, and his pleasure is to drag words this way and that, the argument will prove to him, that he is not making a worthy use of his faculties; for there is no charm in such puzzles, and there is no difficulty in detecting them; but we can tell him of something else the pursuit of which is noble and also difficult.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> A thing of which I have already spoken; — letting alone these puzzles as involving no difficulty, he should be able to follow and criticize in detail every argument, and when a man says that the same is in a manner other, or that other is the same, to understand and refute him from his own point of view, and in the same respect in which he asserts either of these affections. But to show that somehow and in some sense the same is other, or the other same, or the great small, or the like unlike; and to delight in always bringing forward such contradictions, is no real refutation, but is clearly the new-born babe of some one who is only beginning to approach the problem of being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> For certainly, my friend, the attempt to separate all existences from one another is a barbarism and utterly unworthy of an educated or philosophical mind.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The attempt at universal separation is the final annihilation of all reasoning; for only by the union of conceptions with one another do we attain to discourse of reason.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, observe that we were only just in time in making a resistance to such separatists, and compelling them to admit that one thing mingles with another.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Why so?","st":"259d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Why, that we might be able to assert discourse to be a kind of being; for if we could not, the worst of all consequences would follow; we should have no philosophy. Moreover, the necessity for determining the nature of discourse presses upon us at this moment; if utterly deprived of it, we could no more hold discourse; and deprived of it we should be if we admitted that there was no admixture of natures at all.","st":"259d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true. But I do not understand why at this moment we must determine the nature of discourse.","st":"259d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Perhaps you will see more clearly by the help of the following explanation.","st":"259d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What explanation?","st":"259d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Not-being has been acknowledged by us to be one among many classes diffused over all being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And thence arises the question, whether not-being mingles with opinion and language.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> If not-being has no part in the proposition, then all things must be true; but if not-being has a part, then false opinion and false speech are possible, for to think or to say what is not — is falsehood, which thus arises in the region of thought and in speech.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That is quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And where there is falsehood surely there must be deceit.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And if there is deceit, then all things must be full of idols and images and fancies.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Into that region the Sophist, as we said, made his escape, and, when he had got there, denied the very possibility of falsehood; no one, he argued, either conceived or uttered falsehood, inasmuch as not-being did not in any way partake of being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now, not-being has been shown to partake of being, and therefore he will not continue fighting in this direction, but he will probably say that some ideas partake of not-being, and some not, and that language and opinion are of the non-partaking class; and he will still fight to the death against the existence of the image-making and phantastic art, in which we have placed him, because, as he will say, opinion and language do not partake of not-being, and unless this participation exists, there can be no such thing as falsehood. And, with the view of meeting this evasion, we must begin by enquiring into the nature of language, opinion, and imagination, in order that when we find them we may find also that they have communion with not-being, and, having made out the connexion of them, may thus prove that falsehood exists; and therein we will imprison the Sophist, if he deserves it, or, if not, we will let him go again and look for him in another class.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly, Stranger, there appears to be truth in what was said about the Sophist at first, that he was of a class not easily caught, for he seems to have abundance of defences, which he throws up, and which must every one of them be stormed before we can reach the man himself. And even now, we have with difficulty got through his first defence, which is the not-being of not-being, and lo! here is another; for we have still to show that falsehood exists in the sphere of language and opinion, and there will be another and another line of defence without end.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Any one, Theaetetus, who is able to advance even a little ought to be of good cheer, for what would he who is dispirited at a little progress do, if he were making none at all, or even undergoing a repulse? Such a faint heart, as the proverb says, will never take a city: but now that we have succeeded thus far, the citadel is ours, and what remains is easier.","st":"261a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"261a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, as I was saying, let us first of all obtain a conception of language and opinion, in order that we may have clearer grounds for determining, whether not-being has any concern with them, or whether they are both always true, and neither of them ever false.","st":"261a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, now, let us speak of names, as before we were speaking of ideas and letters; for that is the direction in which the answer may be expected.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> And what is the question at issue about names?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The question at issue is whether all names may be connected with one another, or none, or only some of them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Clearly the last is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I understand you to say that words which have a meaning when in sequence may be connected, but that words which have no meaning when in sequence cannot be connected?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are you saying?","st":"261c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> What I thought that you intended when you gave your assent; for there are two sorts of intimation of being which are given by the voice.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> One of them is called nouns, and the other verbs.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Describe them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> That which denotes action we call a verb.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the other, which is an articulate mark set on those who do the actions, we call a noun.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> A succession of nouns only is not a sentence, any more than of verbs without nouns.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I do not understand you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I see that when you gave your assent you had something else in your mind. But what I intended to say was, that a mere succession of nouns or of verbs is not discourse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"261e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I mean that words like ‘walks,’ ‘runs,’ ‘sleeps,’ or any other words which denote action, however many of them you string together, do not make discourse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How can they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Or, again, when you say ‘lion,’ ‘stag,’ ‘horse,’ or any other words which denote agents — neither in this way of stringing words together do you attain to discourse; for there is no expression of action or inaction, or of the existence of existence or non-existence indicated by the sounds, until verbs are mingled with nouns; then the words fit, and the smallest combination of them forms language, and is the simplest and least form of discourse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Again I ask, What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When any one says ‘A man learns,’ should you not call this the simplest and least of sentences?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Yes, for he now arrives at the point of giving an intimation about something which is, or is becoming, or has become, or will be. And he not only names, but he does something, by connecting verbs with nouns; and therefore we say that he discourses, and to this connexion of words we give the name of discourse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And as there are some things which fit one another, and other things which do not fit, so there are some vocal signs which do, and others which do not, combine and form discourse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is another small matter.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> A sentence must and cannot help having a subject.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And must be of a certain quality.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now let us mind what we are about.","st":"262d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> We must do so.","st":"262d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I will repeat a sentence to you in which a thing and an action are combined, by the help of a noun and a verb; and you shall tell me of whom the sentence speaks.","st":"262d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I will, to the best of my power.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> ‘Theaetetus sits’ — not a very long sentence.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Not very.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Of whom does the sentence speak, and who is the subject? that is what you have to tell.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Of me; I am the subject.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Or this sentence, again —","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What sentence?","st":"262e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> ‘Theaetetus, with whom I am now speaking, is flying.’","st":"262e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> That also is a sentence which will be admitted by every one to speak of me, and to apply to me.","st":"262e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We agreed that every sentence must necessarily have a certain quality.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what is the quality of each of these two sentences?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The one, as I imagine, is false, and the other true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The true says what is true about you?","st":"263a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the false says what is other than true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And therefore speaks of things which are not as if they were?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And say that things are real of you which are not; for, as we were saying, in regard to each thing or person, there is much that is and much that is not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The second of the two sentences which related to you was first of all an example of the shortest form consistent with our definition.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, this was implied in recent admission.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, in the second place, it related to a subject?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Who must be you, and can be nobody else?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Unquestionably.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And it would be no sentence at all if there were no subject, for, as we proved, a sentence which has no subject is impossible.","st":"263c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"263c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When other, then, is asserted of you as the same, and not-being as being, such a combination of nouns and verbs is really and truly false discourse.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And therefore thought, opinion, and imagination are now proved to exist in our minds both as true and false.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You will know better if you first gain a knowledge of what they are, and in what they severally differ from one another.","st":"263d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Give me the knowledge which you would wish me to gain.","st":"263d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Are not thought and speech the same, with this exception, that what is called thought is the unuttered conversation of the soul with herself?","st":"263d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But the stream of thought which flows through the lips and is audible is called speech?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"263e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And we know that there exists in speech...","st":"263e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What exists?","st":"263e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Affirmation.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, we know it.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When the affirmation or denial takes Place in silence and in the mind only, have you any other name by which to call it but opinion?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There can be no other name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And when opinion is presented, not simply, but in some form of sense, would you not call it imagination?","st":"264a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And seeing that language is true and false, and that thought is the conversation of the soul with herself, and opinion is the end of thinking, and imagination or phantasy is the union of sense and opinion, the inference is that some of them, since they are akin to language, should have an element of falsehood as well as of truth?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Do you perceive, then, that false opinion and speech have been discovered sooner than we expected? — For just now we seemed to be undertaking a task which would never be accomplished.","st":"264b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I perceive.","st":"264b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then let us not be discouraged about the future; but now having made this discovery, let us go back to our previous classification.","st":"264b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What classification?","st":"264c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> We divided image-making into two sorts; the one likeness-making, the other imaginative or phantastic.","st":"264c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"264c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And we said that we were uncertain in which we should place the Sophist.","st":"264c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> We did say so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And our heads began to go round more and more when it was asserted that there is no such thing as an image or idol or appearance, because in no manner or time or place can there ever be such a thing as falsehood.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"264d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And now, since there has been shown to be false speech and false opinion, there may be imitations of real existences, and out of this condition of the mind an art of deception may arise.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite possible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And we have already admitted, in what preceded, that the Sophist was lurking in one of the divisions of the likeness-making art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us, then, renew the attempt, and in dividing any class, always take the part to the right, holding fast to that which holds the Sophist, until we have stripped him of all his common properties, and reached his difference or peculiar. Then we may exhibit him in his true nature, first to ourselves and then to kindred dialectical spirits.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> You may remember that all art was originally divided by us into creative and acquisitive.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And the Sophist was flitting before us in the acquisitive class, in the subdivisions of hunting, contests, merchandize, and the like.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> But now that the imitative art has enclosed him, it is clear that we must begin by dividing the art of creation; for imitation is a kind of creation — of images, however, as we affirm, and not of real things.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> In the first place, there are two kinds of creation.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"265b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> One of them is human and the other divine.","st":"265b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I do not follow.","st":"265b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Every power, as you may remember our saying originally, which causes things to exist, not previously existing, was defined by us as creative.","st":"265b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I remember.","st":"265b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Looking, now, at the world and all the animals and plants, at things which grow upon the earth from seeds and roots, as well as at inanimate substances which are formed within the earth, fusile or non-fusile, shall we say that they come into existence — not having existed previously — by the creation of God, or shall we agree with vulgar opinion about them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What is it?","st":"265c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The opinion that nature brings them into being from some spontaneous and unintelligent cause. Or shall we say that they are created by a divine reason and a knowledge which comes from God?","st":"265c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I dare say that, owing to my youth, I may often waver in my view, but now when I look at you and see that you incline to refer them to God, I defer to your authority.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Nobly said, Theaetetus, and if I thought that you were one of those who would hereafter change your mind, I would have gently argued with you, and forced you to assent; but as I perceive that you will come of yourself and without any argument of mine, to that belief which, as you say, attracts you, I will not forestall the work of time. Let me suppose, then, that things which are said to be made by nature are the work of divine art, and that things which are made by man out of these are works of human art. And so there are two kinds of making and production, the one human and the other divine.","st":"265d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, now, subdivide each of the two sections which we have already.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> How do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I mean to say that you should make a vertical division of production or invention, as you have already made a lateral one.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> I have done so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, now, there are in all four parts or segments — two of them have reference to us and are human, and two of them have reference to the gods and are divine.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And, again, in the division which was supposed to be made in the other way, one part in each subdivision is the making of the things themselves, but the two remaining parts may be called the making of likenesses; and so the productive art is again divided into two parts.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Tell me the divisions once more.","st":"266a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> I suppose that we, and the other animals, and the elements out of which things are made — fire, water, and the like — are known by us to be each and all the creation and work of God.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And there are images of them, which are not them, but which correspond to them; and these are also the creation of a wonderful skill.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What are they?","st":"266b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The appearances which spring up of themselves in sleep or by day, such as a shadow when darkness arises in a fire, or the reflection which is produced when the light in bright and smooth objects meets on their surface with an external light, and creates a perception the opposite of our ordinary sight.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes; and the images as well as the creation are equally the work of a divine hand.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what shall we say of human art? Do we not make one house by the art of building, and another by the art of drawing, which is a sort of dream created by man for those who are awake?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And other products of human creation are also twofold and go in pairs; there is the thing, with which the art of making the thing is concerned, and the image, with which imitation is concerned.","st":"266c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Now I begin to understand, and am ready to acknowledge that there are two kinds of production, and each of them twofold; in the lateral division there is both a divine and a human production; in the vertical there are realities and a creation of a kind of similitudes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And let us not forget that of the imitative class the one part was to have been likeness-making, and the other phantastic, if it could be shown that falsehood is a reality and belongs to the class of real being.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"266d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And this appeared to be the case; and therefore now, without hesitation, we shall number the different kinds as two.","st":"266d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Then, now, let us again divide the phantastic art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Where shall we make the division?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is one kind which is produced by an instrument, and another in which the creator of the appearance is himself the instrument.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> When any one makes himself appear like another in his figure or his voice, imitation is the name for this part of the phantastic art.","st":"266e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let this, then, be named the art of mimicry, and this the province assigned to it; as for the other division, we are weary and will give that up, leaving to some one else the duty of making the class and giving it a suitable name.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Let us do as you say — assign a sphere to the one and leave the other.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There is a further distinction, Theaetetus, which is worthy of our consideration, and for a reason which I will tell you.","st":"267a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Let me hear.","st":"267a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> There are some who imitate, knowing what they imitate, and some who do not know. And what line of distinction can there possibly be greater than that which divides ignorance from knowledge?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There can be no greater.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Was not the sort of imitation of which we spoke just now the imitation of those who know? For he who would imitate you would surely know you and your figure?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Naturally.","st":"267b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what would you say of the figure or form of justice or of virtue in general? Are we not well aware that many, having no knowledge of either, but only a sort of opinion, do their best to show that this opinion is really entertained by them, by expressing it, as far as they can, in word and deed?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Yes, that is very common.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And do they always fail in their attempt to be thought just, when they are not? Or is not the very opposite true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The very opposite.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Such a one, then, should be described as an imitator — to be distinguished from the other, as he who is ignorant is distinguished from him who knows?","st":"267c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> True.","st":"267c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Can we find a suitable name for each of them? This is clearly not an easy task; for among the ancients there was some confusion of ideas, which prevented them from attempting to divide genera into species; wherefore there is no great abundance of names. Yet, for the sake of distinctness, I will make bold to call the imitation which coexists with opinion, the imitation of appearance — that which coexists with science, a scientific or learned imitation.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Granted.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> The former is our present concern, for the Sophist was classed with imitators indeed, but not among those who have knowledge.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Let us, then, examine our imitator of appearance, and see whether he is sound, like a piece of iron, or whether there is still some crack in him.","st":"267d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Let us examine him.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Indeed there is a very considerable crack; for if you look, you find that one of the two classes of imitators is a simple creature, who thinks that he knows that which he only fancies; the other sort has knocked about among arguments, until he suspects and fears that he is ignorant of that which to the many he pretends to know.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> There are certainly the two kinds which you describe.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Shall we regard one as the simple imitator — the other as the dissembling or ironical imitator?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Very good.","st":"267e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And shall we further speak of this latter class as having one or two divisions?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Answer yourself.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Upon consideration, then, there appear to me to be two; there is the dissembler, who harangues a multitude in public in a long speech, and the dissembler, who in private and in short speeches compels the person who is conversing with him to contradict himself.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> What you say is most true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And who is the maker of the longer speeches? Is he the statesman or the popular orator?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The latter.","st":"268a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> And what shall we call the other? Is he the philosopher or the Sophist?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> The philosopher he cannot be, for upon our view he is ignorant; but since he is an imitator of the wise he will have a name which is formed by an adaptation of the word sophos. What shall we name him? I am pretty sure that I cannot be mistaken in terming him the true and very Sophist.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> Shall we bind up his name as we did before, making a chain from one end of his genealogy to the other?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> By all means.","st":"268b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Stranger:<\/span> He, then, who traces the pedigree of his art as follows — who, belonging to the conscious or dissembling section of the art of causing self-contradiction, is an imitator of appearance, and is separated from the class of phantastic which is a branch of image-making into that further division of creation, the juggling of words, a creation human, and not divine — any one who affirms the real Sophist to be of this blood and lineage will say the very truth.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Theaetetus:<\/span> Undoubtedly.","st":"268d"}],[]);})();
